    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "While the importance of positivity in romantic relationships has been an enduring finding in research on Western populations, there is reason to believe that high positivity in relationships is less important for relationship health in East Asian contexts, where positive affective states are less highly valued. 60 Japanese and 60 European American couples were recruited, and each participant was asked to share about a negative event as their partner offered support. We examined the effect of culture on two constructs: 1) valence of facial expressions, used to determine the amount of expressed positive vs. negative affect in a romantic couple’s interaction, and 2) perceived positivity resonance, a measure of a romantic partner’s belief in their partner’s connectedness and supportiveness (Fredrickson, 2016). Exploring the effect of culture on the links between these and couples’ moods after conversation and relationship satisfaction, we found a cultural difference in the effect of partners’ valence on a speaker's mood after a negative conversation. Perceived positivity resonance, on the other hand, was positively linked with better mood after conversations and better relationship satisfaction across both cultures.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Maceda, D., Cachia, J., Uchida, Y., and Tsai, J. (2024). Cultural Differences in Romantic Support During Negative Conversations. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/kf925qx5700.",
